 ## Diagnostic and integration boundary
 
 The `producer_memory` example takes an assembled copy of the repository-authored
-metal2vulkan `runtime_examples.ll` fixture. Its canonical-only mode exercises
-upload, compute, independent readback, CPU visibility, export lifetime and
-poisoning without assuming an opaque-FD POSIX layout:
+metal2vulkan `runtime_examples.ll` fixture. Its default mode imports a duplicate
+of the opaque FD back into the same Vulkan device and maps that imported memory.
+It uses the producer's exact allocation size, memory type and allocation flags;
+the driver interprets the handle. No second runtime buffer or data copy creates
+the alias. This host diagnostic requires coherent host-visible memory so both
+CPU mappings share one coherent view; the noncoherent native provider remains a
+separately qualified device path.
 
 ```sh
 cargo run -p reims-vgpu-vulkan --example producer_memory -- \
-    /path/to/runtime_examples.air --canonical-only
+    /path/to/runtime_examples.air --vulkan-alias
 ```
 
-Without `--canonical-only`, it additionally requires and checks a complete
-POSIX mapping before registering selected aliases, then checks alias writes
-against the canonical mapping. Failure is not a native-alias qualification.
+The complete initial byte layout is checked before registration. The test then
+resolves a span across both mappings, rejects repeated physical pages, checks
+alias writes through the canonical address, drops the alias, and rejects its
+stale address. Runtime compute and independent readback check the full allocation.
+The imported mapping is unmapped and freed before the CPU epoch can end.
+
+`--canonical-only` exercises upload, compute, independent readback, CPU visibility,
+export lifetime and poisoning without an additional CPU mapping. `--posix-alias`
+retains the explicit driver-specific `mmap` probe: it requires and checks a
+complete POSIX layout before registration or writes, and refuses a mismatch.
+A successful Vulkan import does not qualify that separate POSIX interpretation.
 The example's 32 KiB geometry and five-second waits are diagnostic choices.
 
+The [opaque handle contract](https://docs.vulkan.org/refpages/latest/refpages/source/VkExternalMemoryHandleTypeFlagBits.html)
+does not promise a generic memory-file layout. The
+[import contract](https://docs.vulkan.org/refpages/latest/refpages/source/VkImportMemoryFdInfoKHR.html)
+transfers ownership of the duplicated FD to Vulkan only on a successful import;
+the example closes it itself on failure.
+
 General guest integration still needs shared device ownership, producer-backed
 QEMU region allocation, native mapping updates, guest gather/remap registration,
 resource lifetime hooks, and CPU/GPU handoffs at every guest-visible event.
